WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Every cloud deployment script runs with an identity.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:13.960
That identity may come from a human session, service account, role assumption, workload identity, managed identity, deployment agent, or CI/CD runner credential.

3
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:21.580
The identity determines what the script can read, create, update, publish, route, or disable.

4
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:27.200
Credentials are therefore not setup details; they are part of the deployment authority model.

5
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:35.620
Hard-coded secrets, command examples, logs, and broadly accessible configuration can spread deployment credentials beyond the authorized job.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:41.580
Least privilege should be based on action, environment, service, and repository.

7
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.240
A validation job does not need the same permissions as a production deployment.

8
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.020
A staging rollout should not inherit production access.

9
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.240
A package publication job may need registry permissions but not infrastructure administration.

10
00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:00.780
A rollback path may require narrowly scoped authority that is different from ordinary release activity.

11
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.000
Splitting those permissions reduces unnecessary access and makes failures easier to contain.

12
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.080
Credential delivery should use approved mechanisms.

13
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:17.180
In CI/CD, that may mean scoped secrets, short-lived credentials, workload identity, or managed identity where the platform supports it.

14
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:24.400
Locally, it means avoiding hidden assumptions about broad profiles and making the target identity visible enough to validate.

15
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:33.140
Secrets should not be hard-coded into scripts, embedded in command examples, written into logs, or stored in broadly accessible configuration.

16
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:40.140
Broad shared administrator credentials may make automation quick to start, but they make incidents harder to understand and contain.

17
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:48.720
Secure deployment identities have owners, documented purpose, rotation paths, revocation steps, and periodic access review.

18
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:55.940
Teams should be able to answer which scripts use a credential, what it can touch, and how to disable it quickly if something goes wrong.
